<h2 id='who-its-for'>🎯 Who Cloudways Is (and Isn't) For</h2>

<p><strong>Cloudways is the right call if:</strong> You're running a WooCommerce or Magento store doing meaningful revenue and you've outgrown shared hosting. You want managed infrastructure without hiring a DevOps engineer. You need flexibility to scale server size without migrating to a new host. Your traffic is unpredictable — you run influencer campaigns, appear on press, or do seasonal flash sales that could 10x your normal traffic overnight.</p>

<p>It also makes sense for <a href='https://ecommerceoperator.io/operations/ecommerce-tech-stack'>agencies managing multiple client storefronts</a> who need a single control plane with per-client isolation and team access controls.</p>

<p><strong>Cloudways is the wrong call if:</strong> You need a fully managed solution where you never think about servers at all (look at Shopify's infrastructure or WP Engine). Your store is built on Shopify already — Cloudways is irrelevant for SaaS ecommerce platforms. You need built-in email hosting. You're running a simple brochure site or low-traffic store where the cost-to-value ratio doesn't make sense — a $14/mo DigitalOcean Droplet you manage yourself would serve you better.</p>

<div class='callout-blue'>
  <strong>Tip:</strong> If you're evaluating Cloudways against WP Engine for a WooCommerce store, the key question is traffic predictability. WP Engine's flat pricing is easier to budget; Cloudways Autonomous wins on handling unpredictable spikes. For stores with steady, predictable traffic, WP Engine's developer experience and staging workflow are often smoother.
</div>

